<h1>Route Constraints in ASP.NET Core MVC</h1>

<p>
    Route Constraints are used to restrict and validate
    route parameter values in URLs.
</p>

<p>
    They help ASP.NET Core determine whether
    a route should match a request or not.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why Route Constraints are Used?</h2>

<p>
    Route constraints help:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Prevent invalid URLs</li>
    <li>Improve route matching</li>
    <li>Validate route parameters</li>
    <li>Create safer APIs</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Basic Example Without Constraint</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{id}")]
public IActionResult Details(int id)
{
    return Content($"Id: {id}");
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Problem:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/abc
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Here:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
abc cannot be converted to int
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This may create model binding issues.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Using int Constraint</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
public IActionResult Details(int id)
{
    return Content($"Id: {id}");
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Now:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/5
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Valid
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/abc
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Invalid
</p>

<hr />

<h2>How int Constraint Works</h2>

<p>
    ASP.NET Core checks:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
Can route value be converted to integer?
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    If yes:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Route matches successfully</li>
</ul>

<p>
    If no:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Route does not match</li>
    <li>404 Not Found may occur</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Common Route Constraints</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Constraint</th>
        <th>Description</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>int</td>
        <td>Only integers allowed</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>guid</td>
        <td>Only GUID values allowed</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>alpha</td>
        <td>Only alphabets allowed</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>bool</td>
        <td>Only true/false allowed</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>datetime</td>
        <td>Only valid date values allowed</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>GUID Constraint Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{id:guid}")]
public IActionResult Get(Guid id)
{
    return Content(id.ToString());
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Example valid URL:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/2f1c4f67-8a2d-4b3c-91aa-2d9a4d5e8f33
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Alpha Constraint Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{name:alpha}")]
public IActionResult GetByName(string name)
{
    return Content(name);
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Valid:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/iphone
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Invalid:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/iphone123
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Minimum Length Constraint</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{name:minlength(3)}")]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Here:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Minimum 3 characters required</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Maximum Length Constraint</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{name:maxlength(10)}")]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Here:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Maximum 10 characters allowed</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Range Constraint</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{id:range(1,100)}")]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Only values between:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
1 to 100
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    are allowed.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Multiple Constraints</h2>

<p>
    Multiple constraints can be combined together.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{id:int:min(1)}")]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Here:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Value must be integer</li>
    <li>Value must be greater than 0</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Regex Constraint</h2>

<p>
    Route constraints also support regular expressions.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{code:regex(^[A-Z]{3}[0-9]{3}$)}")]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Valid Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
ABC123
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>How Route Constraints Work Internally</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Incoming URL
      ↓
Routing Middleware
      ↓
Constraint Validation
      ↓
Route Match Success/Failure
      ↓
Action Execution
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>What Happens if Constraint Fails?</h2>

<p>
    If route value does not satisfy the constraint:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Route is ignored</li>
    <li>Action method is not executed</li>
    <li>404 Not Found may occur</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Route Constraints vs Model Validation</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Route Constraints</th>
        <th>Model Validation</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Validates URL route values</td>
        <td>Validates model data</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Runs during route matching</td>
        <td>Runs after model binding</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Prevents invalid route execution</td>
        <td>Prevents invalid data processing</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>Advantages of Route Constraints</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Cleaner route matching</li>
    <li>Safer APIs</li>
    <li>Better URL validation</li>
    <li>Reduces invalid requests</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Points</h2>

<ul>
    <li>
        Route constraints validate URL parameters during route matching.
    </li>

    <li>
        Constraints help prevent invalid routes from executing actions.
    </li>

    <li>
        int, guid, alpha, minlength are common route constraints.
    </li>

    <li>
        If constraint validation fails,
        route matching fails.
    </li>

    <li>
        Route constraints execute before model validation.
    </li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Final Conclusion</h2>

<p>
    Route Constraints help ASP.NET Core MVC applications
    validate and restrict route parameter values,
    improving route safety and request handling.
</p>
